<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shinsengumi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1869">1869</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shinsengumi was a special police force of masterless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1863 to protect the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s representatives. Under the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-katamori">Matsudaira Katamori</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a> and led by Kondō Isami and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijikata-toshizo">Hijikata Toshizō</a>, it hunted down pro-imperial radicals, most famously in the Ikedaya incident of 1864. It fought for the shogunate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> until its last members surrendered in 1869.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shinsengumi.jpg" alt="Shinsengumi" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Washiucho · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Shinsengumi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s forced opening to the West in 1854 increased internal instability, and loyalists, particularly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Chōshū Domain</a>, began to rebel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonno-joi">sonnō jōi</a>, 'revere the emperor, expel the barbarians'. In response the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> formed the Rōshigumi on 19 October 1862, a squad of 234 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronin">rōnin</a> drawn from the sword schools of Edo, to protect the 14th shōgun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemochi">Tokugawa Iemochi</a>, on a journey to Kyoto to meet Emperor Kōmei. The squad's leader, Kiyokawa Hachirō, who was loyal to the emperor, planned to gather other rōnin to police the city, and when this scheme came out the squad was ordered back to Edo.</p><p>Nineteen members, mainly from the Mito clan, stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and formed the Mibu Rōshigumi. On 18 August 1863 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-katamori">Matsudaira Katamori</a>, who had judged that the mission should become patrolling Kyoto's streets in the name of the Tokugawa bakufu, renamed it the Shinsengumi, meaning Newly Selected Corps.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Early on there was internal strife. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-katamori">Matsudaira Katamori</a> ordered the killing of the erratic Serizawa Kamo, and in October 1863 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijikata-toshizo">Hijikata Toshizō</a> led a group that assassinated him. On 8 July 1864, in the Ikedaya incident, thirty Shinsengumi suppressed a cell of twenty Chōshū revolutionaries, possibly preventing the burning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>; the incident led to soldiers enlisting in the squad.</p><p>In January 1868 they fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-toba-fushimi">Battle of Toba–Fushimi</a>. Reformed as the Kōyō Chinbutai, they lost the Battle of Kōshū-Katsunuma on 29 March 1868. Kondō was captured by Imperial forces and beheaded on 17 May 1868. Hijikata went on to fight at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a> and, in December 1868, joined the Republic of Ezo in the north. He was killed by a gunshot at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakodate">Hakodate</a> on 20 June 1869, and the last commander, Sōma Kazue, surrendered with the survivors three days later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>At its peak the Shinsengumi had about 300 members. It was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> group of the Tokugawa era to admit people from non-samurai classes such as farmers and merchants, though it is a misconception that most members were of those classes: of 106 members whose origins are recorded, 87 were samurai. Its code, created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijikata-toshizo">Hijikata Toshizō</a>, had five articles, forbidding deviation from the samurai code, leaving the group, raising money privately, taking part in others' litigation and engaging in private fights. The penalty for breaking any rule was seppuku. Its first uniform was a light-blue haori with white mountain stripes. After about a year the corps appears to have adopted an all-black uniform instead.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shinsengumi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Kyoto, the <b>Shinsengumi</b>, the Bakufu's special police force, fought an armed encounter with shishi, among them masterless samurai of the Chōshū, Tosa and Higo domains, at the Ikedaya Inn.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-8">8 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1862 Matsudaira Katamori became Kyoto protector, policing the capital with the <b>Shinsengumi</b> and fighting Choshu radicals.</blockquote><a href="/state/aizu-domain">Aizu Domain</a></li></ul></section>
